.nh
.TH rcvd 1 "21 July 2026" "rcvd 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rcvd - Resilient, Cryptographic, Verifiable DNS

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrcvd\fP [\fB-config\fP \fIpath\fP] [\fIflag\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBrcvd\fP is a single statically-linked binary that performs privacy-first encrypted DNS
forwarding and serving. Its defining invariant: every query that leaves the host does so over
an encrypted transport. If every configured encrypted upstream fails, \fBrcvd\fP returns SERVFAIL
rather than falling back to cleartext DNS. Port 53 is never a plaintext egress: \fBrcvd\fP may
accept plaintext queries on a \fBloopback\fP port 53 (Mode 1), but every query leaving the host
is encrypted, and binding port 53 on a non-loopback interface is rejected at startup.

.PP
\fBrcvd\fP operates in two modes, selectable per configuration file:

.PP
\fBMode 1 (Forwarder):\fP Accepts plain DNS from local or LAN clients (loopback or configured
listen address) and forwards each query upstream over an encrypted transport (DoQ, DoT, or
DoH). The client-to-rcvd link is plaintext; the off-host leg is always encrypted.

.PP
\fBMode 2 (Upstream Service):\fP Exposes encrypted DoH, DoT, and/or DoQ endpoints that other
resolvers or browsers query directly. Both ends of the query path are then encrypted. TLS
certificates are provided via auto-generated self-signed certificate (testing),
operator-supplied cert/key, or certmagic ACME automation.

.PP
Because both the forwarding and serving legs are encrypted, two or more \fBrcvd\fP instances in
different locations can be chained - a Mode 1 forwarder pointing at another instance's Mode
2 endpoint - with the off-host link encrypted end to end.

.PP
Both modes may run simultaneously from a single process, sharing one in-memory cache.

.PP
All flags are boolean (they take no value argument). The config file path is the one exception
and uses \fB-config\fP \fIpath\fP\&. Passing a path as a bare positional argument is an error — see
\fBCOMMON MISTAKES\fP below.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-config\fP \fIpath\fP

.PP
Path to the TOML configuration file. Defaults to \fBrcvd.toml\fP in the current directory. For system deployments use the full path, e.g. \fB/etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml\fP\&.

.PP
\fB-stats\fP

.PP
Connect to the running instance's Unix socket and print aggregate statistics, then exit. Requires \fBstats_enabled = true\fP in the configuration file. The socket path is derived from the config file name.

.PP
\fB-audit\fP

.PP
Connect to the running instance's Unix socket and print the live posture report: active listeners, no-cleartext invariant confirmation, and cache type. Requires \fBstats_enabled = true\fP\&. The posture reflects the running process's loaded configuration — not just the file on disk — confirming what is actually in force.

.PP
\fB-blocklist-reload\fP

.PP
Connect to the running instance's Unix socket and trigger a hot-reload of the blocklist \fBfiles\fP the running process already knows about — that is, the set of \fB[blocklists] files\fP it read when it started — then exit. Requires \fBstats_enabled = true\fP — the Unix control socket this connects to is created by the statistics feature, so with statistics off there is no socket to reach and the command cannot signal the daemon (the blocklist itself still filters normally; only this live-reload verb is unavailable). The reload re-reads those same files from disk, rebuilds the entire in-memory set, and atomically swaps it in, so both \fBadded and removed\fP domains \fIwithin the known files\fP take effect without restarting the process — preserving statistics, cache, and connections. It is asynchronous and non-blocking: the command returns immediately with an acknowledgment while the (potentially minute-long) rescan runs in the background, exactly like the startup load, so DNS is never paused. Final counts are written to the daemon log when the swap completes. Edit the file(s), then run this. Only local \fBfiles\fP are reloaded; \fBupdate_urls\fP are not re-fetched.

.PP
Note: this reloads the \fIcontents\fP of the already-known files only. It does \fBnot\fP re-read the config, so \fBadding a new path to [blocklists] files (or removing one) is NOT picked up by a hot-reload\fP — the running process keeps the file set it started with. Changing the set of blocklist files requires restarting the process so it re-reads the config.

.PP
\fB-verify-upstream\fP

.PP
Load the configuration, probe every configured upstream with a real, \fBCA-validating\fP TLS handshake, and print the certificate chain for each: subject, issuer, SANs, validity window, key type, and SHA-256 fingerprint. Does not require a running instance. Exits non-zero if any upstream probe fails.

.PP
This verb asks only "does the upstream present a CA-valid (Web-PKI or private-CA) certificate for its name?" — it is intentionally \fBnot\fP pin-aware. An upstream that is verified in production by an SPKI \fBpin\fP (a self-signed leg with \fBpinned_pubkey\fP set) will FAIL here with \fIcertificate signed by unknown authority\fP — that is EXPECTED and does not mean the live path is broken. The output flags such an upstream and points to \fB-verify-pin\fP\&. Use \fB-verify-pin\fP to validate a pinned upstream.

.PP
\fB-verify-pin\fP

.PP
Load the configuration and, for every upstream that has \fBpinned_pubkey\fP set, dial it the way the live pinned resolver path does (no CA-chain check; exact SubjectPublicKeyInfo match) and report \fBOK\fP or \fBMISMATCH\fP against the configured pin. Upstreams with no pin are skipped. Does not require a running instance. Exits zero only if every pinned upstream matches — suitable for cron/monitoring to catch a silent key rotation on the far end. This is the AUDIT verb of the pin lifecycle; see \fB-show-pin\fP for bootstrap.

.PP
\fB-show-pin\fP \fIindex\fP

.PP
Probe the upstream at the given \fB0-based index\fP into the \fB[[upstreams]]\fP list, and print JUST its leaf SPKI pin (\fBsha256//BASE64\fP) to standard output — nothing else — ready to paste into that upstream's \fBpinned_pubkey\fP\&. All diagnostics go to standard error, so \fBpin=$(rcvd -show-pin 0 -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml)\fR captures exactly the pin. No pin need be configured (that is the point — you are generating one). Works against a self-signed leg. This is the BOOTSTRAP verb of the pin lifecycle: \fB-show-pin\fP (generate) → \fBpinned_pubkey\fP (configure) → \fB-verify-pin\fP (audit).

.PP
\fB-verify-self\fP

.PP
Probe THIS instance's own Mode-2 listeners (DoH/DoT/DoQ) with a real TLS handshake and print the certificate presented to clients: subject, issuer, SANs, validity window, key type, fingerprint, and the leaf \fBSPKI pin\fP (\fBsha256//BASE64\fP). The pin is the same value a client configures as \fBpinned_pubkey\fP and validates with \fB-verify-pin\fP, so an operator managing both ends can copy it directly from this output. Classifies the cert source as SELF-SIGNED (tls_cert_autogen) or CA-ISSUED (certmagic/ACME or bring-your-own). When \fBtls_automation\fP is configured, the report header shows the DoH hostname (the first \fBallowed_domains\fP entry), which is also used as the default DoH SNI (see \fB-server-name\fP). Requires the instance to be running with Mode 2 enabled. If Mode 2 is disabled, reports that there is nothing to verify.

.PP
\fB-server-name\fP \fIname\fP

.PP
TLS SNI hostname to use when probing with \fB-verify-self\fP\&. For the DoH listener, defaults to the configured DoH hostname (\fBtls_automation\fP \fBallowed_domains\fP) when set, otherwise the hostname derived from the listener's address; DoT/DoQ use the listener-derived hostname. Pass this flag to override — e.g. to force a specific SNI when the instance serves multiple names.

.PP
\fB-version\fP

.PP
Print the version string and build timestamp, then exit.

.PP
\fB-help\fP

.PP
Print a short usage summary, then exit.

.SH COMMON MISTAKES
\fBrcvd\fP action flags are boolean and do not consume a path argument. Passing a path
positionally is silently parsed as a leftover argument and causes an error. The config
path always goes with \fB-config\fP:

.EX
WRONG:  rcvd --stats /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml
RIGHT:  rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -stats

WRONG:  rcvd --verify-self /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml
RIGHT:  rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -verify-self
.EE

.PP
\fBrcvd\fP will print a diagnostic naming the unexpected argument and show the corrected form.

.SH EXAMPLES
\fBRun a Mode-1 forwarder (local host)\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd-resolver.toml
.EE

.PP
\fBRun a Mode-1 LAN-facing router forwarder\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd-router.toml
.EE

.PP
\fBRun a Mode-2 upstream service (DoH/DoT/DoQ endpoint)\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd-upstream.toml
.EE

.PP
\fBQuery live statistics from a running instance\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -stats
.EE

.PP
\fBInspect the live posture of a running instance\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -audit
.EE

.PP
\fBHot-reload the blocklist after editing it (no restart, stats preserved)\fP

.EX
# edit /etc/rcvd/domainswild — add or remove domains
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -blocklist-reload
.EE

.PP
\fBVerify TLS certificates of all configured upstreams (CA validation)\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -verify-upstream
.EE

.PP
\fBGenerate the SPKI pin for the first upstream (bootstrap a pin)\fP

.EX
pin=$(rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -show-pin 0)
# paste $pin into that upstream's pinned_pubkey = "sha256//…"
.EE

.PP
\fBValidate configured pins against the live servers (audit)\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml -verify-pin
.EE

.PP
\fBInspect the TLS certificate this Mode-2 instance presents to clients\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd-upstream.toml -verify-self
.EE

.PP
\fBInspect the cert using a specific hostname (for certmagic on-demand TLS)\fP

.EX
rcvd -config /etc/rcvd/rcvd-upstream.toml -verify-self -server-name doh.example.com
.EE

.SH FILES
\fB/etc/rcvd/\fP

.PP
Default directory for configuration files. Multiple config files are kept here; one is activated per running instance via \fB-config\fP\&.

.PP
\fB/etc/rcvd/rcvd.toml\fP (or symlink)

.PP
The conventionally active configuration file on production deployments.

.PP
\fB/run/rcvd/*\&.sock\fP

.PP
Unix sockets for the statistics and audit interface, one per running instance. The socket name is derived from the config file name (e.g. \fBrcvd.toml\fP → \fBrcvd.sock\fP).

.PP
\fB/var/log/rcvd/rcvd.log\fP

.PP
Default log file (used when \fB[logging] file\fP is unset). Set \fBfile = "stdout"\fP in the \fB[logging]\fP section to log to stdout instead (recommended for containers), or \fBfile = "stderr"\fP\&.

.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is TOML. Strict unknown-key rejection is enforced: any unrecognized key is a
hard startup error rather than a silently-ignored typo.

.PP
Key sections:

.PP
\fB[resolver]\fP — Mode 1 forwarder settings. \fBenabled\fP activates Mode 1 (a config may define
this section without turning it on). \fBlisten\fP sets the address and port (default
\fB127.0.0.1:5300\fP). Binding port 53 on a non-loopback address is rejected at startup.

.PP
\fB[[upstreams]]\fP — One block per upstream. \fBhost\fP is the hostname used for TLS SNI and
certificate validation (required). \fBname\fP is an optional label for statistics output, and
\fBport\fP is \fBrequired\fP and explicit — rcvd assumes no default, so state the port you intend
to reach (typically 853 for DoQ/DoT, 443 for DoH). Each block selects
exactly one encrypted protocol: \fBdoq = true\fP (DNS-over-QUIC, RFC 9250), \fBdot = true\fP
(DNS-over-TLS, RFC 7858), or \fBdoh = true\fP (DNS-over-HTTPS, RFC 8484). For DoH, \fBdoh_path\fP
sets the query path (default \fB/dns-query\fP) and \fBdoh3 = true\fP uses HTTP/3 over QUIC (DoH3)
instead of HTTP/2 over TCP. Multiple blocks provide ordered fallback.

.PP
The optional \fBip\fP field pins the dial target so the upstream needs no name resolution at
startup. Without it, rcvd cannot resolve the hostname itself — it has no cleartext bootstrap
path — so you must supply the address (obtained out-of-band, e.g. with \fBdig\fP or \fBq\fP).
The optional \fBpinned_pubkey\fP field pins the upstream's leaf SubjectPublicKeyInfo as \fBsha256//BASE64\fP
(see \fB-show-pin\fP to generate one and \fB-verify-pin\fP to audit it); when set, that upstream is
validated by exact key match rather than CA chain, enabling a self-signed encrypted leg.

.PP
\fB[upstream_service]\fP — Mode 2 service settings. \fBenabled\fP activates Mode 2. \fBlisten_doh\fP,
\fBlisten_dot\fP, and \fBlisten_doq\fP set the endpoint addresses; \fBdoh3 = true\fP also serves DoH3
(HTTP/3 over QUIC) on the \fBlisten_doh\fP address. TLS is configured via \fBtls_cert_autogen\fP
(self-signed), \fBtls_cert\fP/\fBtls_key\fP (bring-your-own), or \fBtls_automation\fP (certmagic).
With \fBtls_cert_autogen\fP, \fBtls_cert_hosts\fP adds extra hostnames or IPs to the self-signed
certificate's SAN (e.g. a tunnel or LAN name that clients connect by) on top of the auto-derived
listen-address hosts and loopback.

.PP
\fBadvertise_ips\fP lists the public IP addresses clients reach this instance at, published as the
\fBipv4hint\fP/\fBipv6hint\fP of every DDR (RFC 9462) SVCB record rcvd serves for
\fB_dns.resolver.arpa\fP\&. One setting covers all listeners, since each designation names the same
host. When a listener binds a concrete IP literal, that address is used automatically and this
setting is redundant. Set it whenever a listener binds a wildcard address (\fB0.0.0.0\fP or \fB::\fP)
or sits behind NAT, because rcvd cannot then determine the address clients actually dial.

.PP
Setting it matters for mobile discovery: Android's native resolver requires the hints. It reads
the SVCB record and connects to the first hint address directly, never resolving the record's
target name, so a record without hints is discarded and the system falls back to DoT on port 853.
rcvd logs a warning at startup when it advertises DDR without hints. Values must be IPv4 or IPv6
literals — a hostname is rejected at startup — and both families should be listed on a dual-stack
endpoint.

.SH DISCOVERY OF DESIGNATED RESOLVERS (DDR)
When the upstream service runs with a certificate hostname configured, rcvd answers the RFC 9462
discovery probe — an SVCB query for \fB_dns.resolver.arpa\fP — about itself, on every listener. It
advertises one SVCB record per encrypted transport it is actually serving, ordered by the SVCB
priority field: DoH first (priority 1), then DoT (2), then DoQ (3). DoH leads because it is the
transport discovering clients act on and its port 443 survives networks that block port 853.

.PP
The records are synthesized, never fetched. \fB_dns.resolver.arpa\fP is a special-use name that
cannot be delegated in the global DNS, so a resolver answering for itself is the only conformant
implementation — there is nothing to publish in a zone file.

.PP
rcvd serves \fBresolver.arpa\fP as a locally served zone (RFC 9462 section 6.4, RFC 6303). Every
other name and record type under it is answered with NODATA and a SOA for negative caching, and
none of them are forwarded upstream. A resolver with no certificate hostname advertises nothing
but still answers the zone, giving clients an explicit "no designated resolver" signal rather than
a query that leaks to a third party.

.PP
Choosing among the three TLS options:

.PP
\fBtls_automation\fP is the option for any endpoint reached directly by a browser or by a native
mobile DNS client. Browsers refuse a self-signed DoH resolver and fall back silently — the
"add a security exception" flow does not apply to the DoH/TRR path — and native mobile clients
(iOS DNSecure, Android Private DNS) have no exception mechanism at all. Both require a real
domain name and a CA-trusted certificate for that name.

.PP
\fBtls_cert\fP/\fBtls_key\fP is the option for a pinned deployment. The certificate may be
self-signed: a client using \fBpinned_pubkey\fP authenticates the SPKI, not a CA chain. What
matters is that the key is stable on disk, so the pin keeps matching across restarts.

.PP
\fBtls_cert_autogen\fP generates a new key pair at every start. It suits development and testing
(\fBcurl -k\fP, container suites) and deployments behind a TLS-terminating proxy that presents a
real certificate to clients. Because the key moves, the served SPKI changes on every restart:
\fBtls_cert_autogen is incompatible with SPKI pinning\fP, and rcvd refuses to start when it can
detect the combination (Mode 2 autogen while an upstream sets \fBpinned_pubkey\fP). Prefer
\fBtls_cert\fP/\fBtls_key\fP whenever any client pins this instance.

.PP
\fB[tls_automation]\fP — certmagic/ACME settings (when \fBtls_automation = true\fP). \fBchallenge\fP
is \fBhttp\fP (default) or \fBdns01\fP (no inbound port; for tunnels/CGNAT). For \fBdns01\fP, set a
supported \fBdns_provider\fP (MVP: \fBcloudflare\fP) and supply the API token from EXACTLY ONE of
\fBdns_api_token\fP (inline), \fBdns_api_token_env\fP (name of an environment variable), or
\fBdns_api_token_file\fP + \fBdns_api_token_key\fP (a dotenv file rcvd parses; a relative path
resolves next to the config file). Setting more than one token source is a startup error.

.PP
\fB[blocklists]\fP — DNS filtering. \fBenabled\fP (default \fBtrue\fP) turns filtering on or off.
\fBfiles\fP is a list of local blocklist files, each a plain domain list (preferred) or a hosts-file
(auto-detected); wildcard entries (\fB*\&.example.com\fP) are supported. \fBupdate_urls\fP lists remote
blocklists to fetch. A matched name is answered without leaving the host.

.PP
\fB[fallback]\fP — Multi-upstream health and failover. \fBphase1_duration_s\fP (default \fB300\fP) is
the startup window of aggressive health checks before settling into steady state.
\fBphase2_failure_threshold\fP is the number of consecutive failures that marks an upstream DOWN.
\fBhealth_check_interval_s\fP (default \fB30\fP) sets the steady-state probe interval. Together these
drive the ordered fallback across the configured \fB[[upstreams]]\fP\&.

.PP
\fB[cache]\fP — In-memory TTL cache. \fBenabled\fP (default \fBtrue\fP) turns caching on or off.
\fBtype\fP selects a preset: \fBlight\fP, \fBstandard\fP (default), or \fBaggressive\fP (raises the
TTL floor to 300s AND the TTL ceiling to 7 days, and enables negative caching and serve-stale).
The preset is mutually exclusive with the manual \fBttl_min\fP/\fBttl_max\fP/\fBneg_ttl_max\fP/
\fBserve_stale_max_s\fP knobs; setting both is a startup error.

.PP
\fB[dnssec]\fP — DNSSEC validation. Enabled by default. By default it validates against the IANA
root trust anchor \fBembedded\fP in the binary (the public root KSK digest; no setup needed).
\fBroot_key_file\fP overrides this with an operator-supplied anchor — either IANA
\fBroot-anchors.xml\fP (DS) or a BIND-style \fBroot.key\fP (DNSKEY), auto-detected. Point it at the
system anchor (e.g. \fB/var/lib/unbound/root.key\fP) so the OS owns trust-anchor updates,
including the rare root KSK rollover, which rcvd does not yet track automatically (RFC 5011).

.PP
\fB[logging]\fP — \fBlevel\fP is \fBdebug\fP, \fBinfo\fP (default), \fBwarn\fP, or \fBerror\fP\&. \fBformat\fP
is \fBtext\fP (default) or \fBjson\fP\&. \fBfile\fP is the log destination: an explicit path, or the
special values \fBstdout\fP or \fBstderr\fP\&. When unset it defaults to \fB/var/log/rcvd/rcvd.log\fP\&.

.PP
\fBstats_enabled\fP — Set to \fBtrue\fP to enable the Unix socket for \fB-stats\fP and \fB-audit\fP\&.

.PP
See \fBdocs/CONFIG.md\fP in the source tree for full documentation of all keys.

.SH EXIT STATUS
\fB0\fP
	Success.

.PP
\fB1\fP
	Configuration error, upstream probe failure, or runtime error.

.PP
\fB2\fP
	Invalid command-line usage (e.g. unexpected positional argument).

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBdig(1)\fP, \fBdnsmasq(8)\fP, \fBsystemd-resolved(8)\fP, \fBresolvectl(1)\fP

.PP
rcvd source and documentation: \fBhttps://rcvd.net\fP

.SH HISTORY
rcvd was originally created in 2026 as a privacy-first, encryption-mandatory DNS engine,
focusing on the IETF standardized transports: DoT, DoH, and DoQ

